<a id="overview"></a>

SCXML, State Chart XML[^scxml], is the W3C standard notation for statecharts: state
machines extended with nesting, parallel regions and history. An SCXML document names:

* the states, nested as compound states or run side by side as parallel regions,
* the events that move the machine between states, and
* the actions it runs on entry, on exit and on each transition.

Because SCXML is a standard, a machine written or drawn in another statechart tool can come
here as a file. xstate follows the same semantics, with JSON in place of XML.

Part of the [dsys toolkit](dsys.md), the `@fsm` syntax declares a state machine as data, in the shape of an xstate machine config[^xstate], and compiles it to an open [Petri net](petri.md):

```Makefile
load dsys/{{ name }}

@{{ name }}
define door
  {"initial": "closed", "states": {"opened": {"entry": "greet", ...}, ...}}
endef

greet:
	@echo opened by $(door.event)

$(door.send open)
__main__: door.__main__
```

The define is data, targets such as `greet` do the work, and `door.__main__` runs the
machine over the events it was sent. The full config is under [syntax](#syntax), and the
JSON shape under [schema](#schema).

* every leaf state is a place, and the machine's configuration is where its tokens are,
* every event is an open place the environment feeds through the machine's `send` call, and
* a transition fires when its source holds the token and its event holds one.

The same machine reads from an SCXML document, [converted](#scxml) in the Lua guest and
lowered through the same config, so `load ./door.scxml` and `@{{ name }}.import` over XML
declare it too. The [subset](#subset) section lists what of SCXML is supported.

<a id="syntax"></a>

## Syntax

| Form | Declares |
| --- | --- |
| `@{{ name }}` above a define | a machine of the define's name, compiled to a net of the same name, `<name>.__main__` its target |
| `@{{ name }}.target` above a define | the same, and a phony target of the machine's name |
| `@{{ name }}.import` above a define | a file's text as the machine, JSON or scxml |
| `load door.fsm`, `load door.scxml` | the file as a machine named by its stem |
| `@{{ name }}.exec (name)` above a define | a jq program over the compiled net, printed at parse |
| `$(fsm.compile name)` | the compiled net's JSON, without loading it |

A config is a machine object with `initial` and `states`; each state has the keys below.
A target names a sibling by its bare name, a child by a leading dot, or any state by `#`
and its path under the machine, as `#door.closed`. A target or an `initial` may be a list,
one leaf per region of a parallel state below, and the regions it leaves out enter by
their own initials.

| Key | Value |
| --- | --- |
| `initial` | the state entered when this one is, a child or any state below it; required on a compound state |
| `states` | the children, which make the state compound; `"type": "parallel"` makes each child a region active at once |
| `type` | `atomic`, `compound`, `parallel` or `final`; the default follows the keys |
| `on` | each event descriptor and its transition: a target, or an object with `target` |
| `always` | the eventless transition, taken as soon as the state is entered |
| `onDone` | the transition taken when a child final state is entered, or every region of a parallel has ended |
| `entry`, `exit` | targets run when the state is entered and left, in any guest; see [actions](#actions) |
| `invoke` | a service run on entry, `{ "src": target, "onDone": .., "onError": .. }`; see [services](#services) |
| `guard` on a transition | a variable answering by its value, a target by its exit status, or `@state` for a state being active; see [guards](#guards) |

A transition with no target stays in its state and consumes the event. A compound state's
transitions are inherited by its children, the nearest handler winning, and a transition
leaving a parallel state leaves every region.

<a id="actions"></a>

## Actions

`entry` and `exit` name targets, one or a list, in any guest. A transition runs the exits
of the states it leaves, deepest first, then the entries of the states it enters, from the
top, as one step of the net transition. The event that moved the machine is bound as
`<name>.event` before the step runs, so a guest reads it from `amk.var`:

```Makefile
@lua.target
define bolt
  print("bolted on " .. amk.var["door.event"])
endef
```

The start configuration's entries run first when `<name>.__main__` starts, with no event
bound. A targetless transition runs nothing. A transition to the state it is in leaves and
re-enters it, as xstate's external self-transition does.

<a id="services"></a>

## Services

`invoke` runs its `src` target as a step when the state is entered, and its value is the
service's output, read back as `<name>.<state>.output`. The state then takes `onDone`, a
target or a list of guarded alternatives, or `onError` when the service failed. A state
with no `onError` stops the run when its service fails.

```Makefile
"checking": {
  "invoke": { "src": "hinge", "onDone": [
    { "guard": "jammed",  "target": "closed" },
    { "guard": "snapped", "target": "broken" },
    { "target": "open" } ] } }
```

While the service runs, the machine is in the invoking state; once it has answered, the
machine holds its outcome until `onDone` or `onError` moves it, and the state's own event
transitions do not fire in between. Inside a parallel state the other regions go on taking
their events meanwhile, and a transition out of the parallel takes the outcome with it.

<a id="guards"></a>

## Guards

A transition may be a list of alternatives, each with a `guard` and a `target`, taken
first-match in order as scxml and xstate do. A guard is a name, answered one of three ways:

| Guard | Holds when |
| --- | --- |
| a variable | its value is not empty, read in the step's own process with no fork |
| a target | its goal succeeds; make prints its error line when it does not |
| `@state`, a path under the machine | the state is active in the configuration being asked, which is scxml's `In()` |

```Makefile
jammed = $(filter jam,$(door.checking.output))

snapped:
	@test "$(door.checking.output)" = snap
```

An alternative without a guard always holds. When no alternative holds, the event is
consumed and the machine stays, as xstate discards an event no guard lets through. An
eventless `always` with guards needs an unguarded alternative, since a machine that stayed
would ask again at once. Inside a parallel state the question is asked once per
configuration of its regions, so a region's guard reads its siblings' states, and what
each region took is settled together as scxml selects.

<a id="events"></a>

## Events

The environment feeds a machine's events as tokens. The machine's `send` call puts one in
the event's place, and the run fires what the token enables:

```Makefile
$(door.send unlock)
$(door.send open)
$(door.send leave)

__main__: door.__main__
	@echo '[$(door.trace)] [$(door.marking )]'
```

An event descriptor under `on` is a name, a partial wildcard `foo.*` taking every name
under `foo`, or `*` taking any event. The most specific descriptor at the nearest state
handles an event, as xstate selects. A wildcard's place is a sentinel, `ev.foo._` or `ev._`,
and `send` routes a name no descriptor gives to the closest one. A name nothing takes is
dropped, as an scxml queue drops it.

A nested final raises the done event of its parent, `done.state.<path>`, within the same
macrostep, and the parent's `onDone` takes it. Eventless transitions fire before events,
done events before the environment's, so a machine's microsteps run to quiescence before the
next event is read. An scxml descriptor `foo` means `foo` and `foo.*`, and `done.state.<id>`
names the state by its path; the converter maps both.

<a id="scxml"></a>

## SCXML

An scxml document becomes the config through `scxml.lua`, a converter of its own in the Lua
guest: lxsc parses the document, and the converter writes the config from the parse, state
by state. The converter is a call over a define holding the document, and `load` and
`@{{ name }}.import` go through it:

```Makefile
define doc
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml" version="1.0" initial="s0" name="door">
  <state id="s0"><transition event="go" target="done"/></state>
  <final id="done"/>
</scxml>
endef

$(info $(fsm.scxml doc))
```

The config is what the document says in the config's own words:

| Document | Config |
| --- | --- |
| `<scxml name="m">` | the machine, `id` `m`; a document with no name is `machine` |
| `<state>`, `<parallel>`, `<final>`, `<history>` | a state of the id, with `type` where the config needs one; history is then refused by the normalizer |
| `initial` on a state, or its `<initial>` element, else the first child | `initial`, as the absolute id `#m.<path>`; several ids, a list |
| `<transition event="e" target="t">` | an entry under `on` for `e` and for `e.*`, since an scxml descriptor takes every name under it; the target as an absolute id, several as a list; no target, an entry without one |
| `<transition target="t">` with no event | an entry under `always` |
| `event="done.state.x"` | `done.state.<path of x>` |
| `cond="In('x')"` | `guard` `@<path of x>` |
| any other `cond` | `guard` as written, which the normalizer refuses as an expression |

What the config cannot carry is left out and reported on stderr, one line under the
define's name, in document order: the `<datamodel>`, the content of `<onentry>`, `<onexit>`
and transitions with the kinds it held, `<invoke>`, and a top-level `<script>`:

```
door: scxml leaves out datamodel, onentry of s0 (raise assign), invoke in s2
```

Within the Lua guest the same two things are `amk.dsys.scxml.convert(text)`, the config as
JSON and the list of what it left out, and `amk.dsys.scxml.import(name)`, the config of a
make variable's text with the report written.

<a id="reduction"></a>

## Reduction

`fsm.jq` normalizes the config to the definition xstate serializes, checked against xstate's
own machine schema, then compiles it to a net for `petri.load`:

| Machine part | Becomes |
| --- | --- |
| A leaf state | a place of its path under the machine, `closed` or `s0.s01`, holding the token when active |
| The initial configuration | one token per active leaf, through each compound's `initial` and every region of a parallel; a list of initials or targets settles each named leaf and the regions left unnamed by default |
| An event | an open place `ev.<event>`; a wildcard, a sentinel `ev.<prefix>._` or `ev._` |
| A transition under `on` | a net transition `<leaf>.<event>` from the leaf and the event's place to the leaves entered |
| `always` | a transition `<leaf>.always` with no event place, listed first |
| A transition of a parallel state | one per configuration of its regions and event, moving every region's token |
| A nested final | an arc from the entering transition to `ev.done.state.<parent>`, when the entered configuration handles it |
| A top-level final | an acceptance marking; several finals, a [list of them](petri.md#endings); the final drops every event it is sent |
| `entry` and `exit` | the net transition's step, the exits then the entries as its targets, `<name>.event` bound |
| `invoke` | a transition `<leaf>.invoke` into a labeled place `<leaf>.invoked`, its step the service, its label the outcome; `onDone` and `onError` leave that place by the label; inside a parallel state the invoked place is one more position of its region, `<leaf>.invoked` in the configuration's name |
| Guarded alternatives | a picker transition into a labeled place `<leaf>.<event>.choice`, its step a generated target printing the first alternative whose guard holds, the configuration bound as `<name>.config`; one transition per alternative out of it by that label, and one back when none held; inside a parallel state one picker per configuration, printing each guarded region's choice joined by `-` |

The `definition` call reads the definition and `net` the net. The definition is what
xstate's `machine.schema.json` describes, so a conformant xstate tool reads it.

<a id="api"></a>

## API

| Call | Does | Answers |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `<name>.__main__` | runs the machine as its net; typically `__main__: <name>.__main__` | a target |
| `$(<name>.send event)` | feeds one event, routed to its place or the closest wildcard's | nothing |
| `$(<name>.state )` | the active states, leaf paths under the machine | words |
| `$(<name>.event)`, `$(<name>.config)`, `$(<name>.<state>.output)` | the event of the move whose actions are running, the configuration a guard is asked about, and a service's last output | variables |
| `$(<name>.step )` | one firing, or none | the transition fired |
| `$(<name>.definition )` | the normalized definition | JSON |
| `$(fsm.scxml doc)` | the [config](#scxml) of the scxml document in the define `doc`, its drops reported on stderr | JSON |
| `$(<name>.net )` | the compiled net | JSON |
| `$(<name>.marking )`, `$(<name>.trace)`, `$(<name>.exec prog)`, `$(<name>.version )` | as for [any net](petri.md#api) | words |

A run ends when the token reaches a top-level final state. A token left in an event place
keeps the run from ending, so feed a machine the events it takes.

<a id="subset"></a>

## Subset

The lowering takes names, never expressions. What it refuses is named at parse, so a
machine either lowers whole or stops make with the construct:

| Construct | Status |
| --- | --- |
| compound and parallel states, `always`, `onDone`, wildcards, targetless transitions, several finals | lowered |
| `entry`, `exit`, `invoke`, guards by variable, target or `@state`, inside a parallel state too | lowered |
| a `target` or `initial` naming one leaf per region, or a state below a child | lowered |
| an scxml `cond` other than `In()` | refused; a guard is a name, not an expression |
| several targets not one per region of one parallel state | refused |
| `history`, `after`, `context`, `meta`, `always` on the machine itself | outside the subset |
| a parallel state inside a parallel state | refused |

The subset is pinned against the W3C scxml conformance tests' core groups and against lxsc,
an scxml interpreter in the Lua guest, which steps each machine beside the net.

<a id="errors"></a>

## Errors

A body that fails the [schema](#schema) is refused first, as in
`door: does not meet schema/fsm.json: Additional property 'after' not allowed`. The
normalizer and compiler then refuse what a schema cannot say:

| Condition | Message |
| --- | --- |
| `initial` naming no state below | `door: initial nope is no state` |
| A target naming no state | `door.closed: target door.gone2 is no state` |
| Several targets not one per region | `door.closed: targets a b are not one per region of a parallel state` |
| A guard written as an expression | `door.closed: guard x > 1 is an expression, not a name` |
| Guarded `always` with no fallback | `door.closed: an eventless transition with guards needs an unguarded alternative` |
| A history state | `door.h: history is outside the subset` |
| A parallel inside a parallel | `door.p.q: a parallel state inside a parallel state is not lowered yet` |

